\section{Distinguish Functions of Streams From Streams of Functions}

Higher-order Lucid permits functions to be passed to or returned from other functions. This raises a semantic distinction: a fixed function mapping streams to streams is not automatically the same as a context-varying stream whose elements are function values. Types and context dependence must state which is intended. Multidimensional intensional models can encode invocation structure using fresh ``place'' dimensions, rather than requiring a single linear time to represent a call tree.

For typed higher-order programs, one compilation approach repeatedly eliminates parameters into indexical equations. Different function orders may require different place dimensions; variables need not depend on every introduced dimension. This is a translation strategy, not a claim that source functions intrinsically execute as tagged tokens.

\section{Pass a Function Through a Lucid Definition}

A higher-order source fragment applies a supplied function in a recursive product:

\begin{verbatim}
ffac(sq, 3) + ffac(cb, 4)
where
  ffac(H, N) = if N < 1 then 1
               else H(N) * ffac(H, N - 1) fi;
  sq(P) = P * P;
  cb(Q) = Q * Q * Q;
end
\end{verbatim}

The two calls bind \texttt{H} to different functions. A place dimension records which call occurrence supplies an actual parameter, and another dimension can encode application inside the higher-order value. Fresh dimensions prevent this machinery from colliding with source dimensions. \Cref{fig:visual-361} sketches the context product.

