\section{Eliminate One Higher-Order Parameter}

\begin{verbatim}
infer_order_and_types(program)
for each textual call of ffac:
    assign a stable call-site coordinate
replace formal H by an intensional binding
    H = actuals(sq, cb, H)
rewrite each recursive call with a place shift
introduce a fresh place dimension for function application
verify no source dimension is captured
\end{verbatim}

The recursive \texttt{H} entry supplies the same function on recursive descendants of a call. Stable call-site numbering belongs to the generated IR and provenance map; source refactoring may renumber it without changing source meaning.

\section{Compile With Typed Dimensional Dependencies}

The IR should represent function and application nodes, value/dependency edges, context shifts for each place dimension, recursive state as equations rather than hidden stacks, dimension/clock dependencies, higher-order and element types, effects, and provenance through each generated \texttt{actuals}/call node. Compilation can produce function-free indexical equations and tagged eduction, or conventional closures and memoized stream functions.

Legal optimizations include inlining, specialization for \texttt{sq} or \texttt{cb}, and dropping irrelevant place dimensions when effects and dimensional dependence permit. Semantic-preservation risks include duplicating an effectful function value, capturing a source dimension, conflating call-site tags, or specializing recursive calls without preserving termination. A correct lowering must relate each source application/context to the corresponding target context, not merely reproduce results for one observed recursion tree.

\section{Keep the Function Argument Across Recursion}

The two original calls illustrate specialization at distinct textual sites. Add a test that calls both specializations with the same numeric argument \(3\). Then the square family computes \(36\) and the cube family computes \(216\). The equal arguments force a cache-key collision if a lowering memoizes only \texttt{ffac} and \(N\). A call site's identity suffices to select the argument only at sites where the function is statically known; a site that calls a context-dependent function also needs the corresponding binding or context.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lrr}
\hline
\(N\) & With square & With cube \\
\hline
0 & 1 & 1 \\
1 & 1 & 1 \\
2 & 4 & 8 \\
3 & 36 & 216 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

One finite lowering emits named variants, as below. It is an alternative to dynamic place lookup for this closed example, not an implementation of arbitrary higher-order Lucid. The recursion must refer to its own variant after substituting the formal. The base case must not demand any application of the function; this can be tested by passing a partial pure function whose domain excludes zero.

\begin{verbatim}
specialize(body, function_formal, binding):
    key := (body.id, binding.id)
    if variants.contains(key): return variants[key]
    entry := reserve_variant(key)
    variants[key] := entry
    expanded := replace_free_formal(body, function_formal, binding)
    for call in recursive_calls(expanded):
        require binding_for_formal(call) == binding
        redirect(call, entry)
    define_variant(entry, expanded)
    return entry
\end{verbatim}

Reserving the variant before rewriting calls avoids infinite specialization of the recursive body. A function that constructs a new callable at every recursive step cannot use this finite procedure unchanged. Retain a residual representation there and do not assign two distinct environment bindings the same identity just because their code pointer matches. The trace for \(N=3\) should use the same selected function at positions \(3,2,1\) and none at \(0\). Comparing those traces makes an accidental switch of function in the recursive step easier to diagnose than comparing the final product alone.
